::ALG1_U04_L01_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q01

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which set of ordered pairs represents a function?
{
=A. (0, 1), (1, 3), (2, 5)#Correct. Each input appears with only one output.
~B. (1, 2), (1, 4), (2, 6)#Input 1 has two different outputs.
~C. (3, 5), (3, 5), (3, 7)#Input 3 is paired with different outputs.
~D. (2, 1), (2, 2), (4, 3)#Input 2 has two different outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q02

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which relationship is not a function?
{
~A. (1, 4), (2, 4), (3, 4)#Repeated outputs are allowed.
~B. (0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 4)#Each input appears once.
=C. (5, 2), (5, 3), (6, 4)#Correct. Input 5 has two different outputs.
~D. (2, 7), (3, 7), (4, 7)#Repeated outputs do not violate the function rule.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q03

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
What must be true for a relationship to be a function?
{
~A. Each output must have exactly one input#Functions control outputs for each input, not inputs for each output.
=B. Each input must have exactly one output#Correct. This is the definition of a function.
~C. Inputs and outputs must be equal#A function can pair different input and output values.
~D. The graph must be a straight line#Many functions are nonlinear.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q04

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which graph would fail the vertical line test?
{
~A. A nonvertical line#A nonvertical line has one output for each input.
~B. A parabola opening upward#This passes the vertical line test.
~C. A horizontal line#A horizontal line is a function.
=D. A vertical line#Correct. One x-value has many y-values.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q05

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which equation represents a linear function?
{
=A. y = 4x - 1#Correct. This has the form y = mx + b.
~B. y = x^2#This is quadratic.
~C. y = 2^x#This is exponential.
~D. x = 5#This vertical line is not a function of x.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q06::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q06

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which equation represents a quadratic function?
{
~A. y = 3x + 8#This is linear.
=B. y = x^2 - 4#Correct. The x^2 term identifies a quadratic function.
~C. y = 7#This is constant.
~D. y = 2^x#This is exponential.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q07::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q07

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
The table x: 1, 2, 3 and y: 5, 5, 5 represents what kind of relationship?
{
~A. Not a function#Repeated outputs do not make a relationship fail the function test.
~B. A vertical line#The inputs are different, so this is not vertical.
~C. An exponential function#The outputs do not show exponential growth or decay.
=D. A function#Correct. Each input has exactly one output, even though outputs repeat.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q08::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q08

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which table fails the function test?
{
~A. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 3, 4, 5#Each input has one output.
~B. x: 1, 2, 3; y: 9, 9, 9#Repeated outputs are allowed.
~C. x: -1, 0, 1; y: 1, 0, 1#Each input appears once.
=D. x: 2, 2, 3; y: 4, 5, 6#Correct. Input 2 has two different outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q09::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q09

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
What does the vertical line test check?
{
=A. Whether each input has at most one output#Correct. A vertical line represents one input value.
~B. Whether the graph is linear#The vertical line test checks function status, not linearity.
~C. Whether outputs repeat#Repeated outputs can still be functions.
~D. Whether the graph crosses the x-axis#X-intercepts do not determine whether a graph is a function.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q10::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q10

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which situation is a function?
{
~A. One student has two different birth dates#One input would have two outputs.
~B. One package has two different weights at the same time#One input would have conflicting outputs.
~C. One x-value has two y-values#This is the opposite of a function.
=D. Each student ID matches one student name#Correct. Each input ID has exactly one output name.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q11::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q11

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which function type best matches y = |x|?
{
~A. Linear#A linear equation has form y = mx + b without absolute value.
=B. Absolute value#Correct. The absolute value bars identify the function type.
~C. Quadratic#A quadratic function includes x^2.
~D. Exponential#An exponential function has the variable in the exponent.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q12::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q12

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which function type best matches y = 2^x?
{
~A. Linear#Linear functions have constant additive change.
~B. Quadratic#Quadratic functions include x^2.
=C. Exponential#Correct. The variable is in the exponent.
~D. Absolute value#Absolute value functions use absolute value bars.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q13::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q13

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
A graph passes the vertical line test. What can you conclude?
{
=A. It represents a function#Correct. No input is paired with more than one output.
~B. It is always linear#Nonlinear graphs can also pass the vertical line test.
~C. It has no outputs#A function has outputs.
~D. It has no x-values#The test is based on x-values.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q14::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q14

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which mapping is a function?
{
~A. 1 -> 5 and 1 -> 6#Input 1 has two outputs.
~B. 2 -> 7 and 2 -> 8#Input 2 has two outputs.
=C. 1 -> 5, 2 -> 5, 3 -> 6#Correct. Each input has one arrow.
~D. 3 -> 1 and 3 -> 9#Input 3 has two outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q15::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q15

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which statement is true about repeated outputs?
{
~A. They always make a relationship not a function#Repeated outputs are allowed.
~B. They are allowed only when the graph is vertical#Vertical graphs are not functions.
~C. They mean every input is repeated#Repeated outputs do not imply repeated inputs.
=D. They are allowed if each input has only one output#Correct. The function rule focuses on inputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q16::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q16

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which relationship has input 4 paired with two different outputs?
{
~A. (4, 7), (5, 7), (6, 7)#Input 4 appears once.
=B. (4, 1), (4, 2), (5, 3)#Correct. Input 4 has outputs 1 and 2.
~C. (1, 4), (2, 4), (3, 4)#The repeated value is an output.
~D. (0, 4), (1, 5), (2, 6)#Input 4 does not appear.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q17::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q17

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which graph type is not a function of x?
{
=A. A circle#Correct. Some vertical lines cross a circle twice.
~B. A nonvertical line#Each input has one output.
~C. A horizontal line#Each input has the same single output.
~D. An upward-opening parabola#Each input has one output.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q18::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q18

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which equation is not a function of x?
{
~A. y = x + 1#This is a linear function.
=B. x = -3#Correct. This vertical line has many y-values for one x-value.
~C. y = x^2#This is a quadratic function.
~D. y = |x|#This is an absolute value function.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q19::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q19

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which table could represent a linear function?
{
~A. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 1, 2, 4#The output changes are not constant.
~B. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 2, 4, 8#This doubles, so it is not linear.
=C. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 3, 7, 11#Correct. The output increases by 4 each step.
~D. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 5, 5, 6#The output changes are not constant.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q20::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q20

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which table could represent a nonlinear function?
{
~A. x: 0, 0, 1; y: 1, 3, 6#This is not a function because input 0 has two different outputs.
~B. x: 1, 1, 2; y: 3, 4, 5#This is not a function because input 1 repeats with different outputs.
=C. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 1, 4, 9#Correct. Each input has one output and the changes are not constant.
~D. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 3, 6, 9#This is linear because the change is constant.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q21::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q21

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
What is the domain of the relation (2, 5), (3, 7), (4, 9)?
{
=A. 2, 3, 4#Correct. The domain is the set of input values.
~B. 5, 7, 9#These are output values, or the range.
~C. 2, 5, 9#This mixes inputs and outputs.
~D. 3, 7, 4#This mixes inputs and outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q22::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q22

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
What is the range of the relation (2, 5), (3, 7), (4, 9)?
{
~A. 2, 3, 4#These are input values.
~B. 2, 5, 7#This mixes inputs and outputs.
=C. 5, 7, 9#Correct. The range is the set of output values.
~D. 3, 4, 9#This mixes inputs and outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q23::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q23

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which input-output table is a function but not linear?
{
~A. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 2, 5, 8#This is linear because the change is constant.
=B. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 0, 1, 4#Correct. Each input has one output, but the changes are not constant.
~C. x: 1, 1, 2; y: 4, 5, 6#This is not a function.
~D. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 3, 3, 3#This is a constant linear function.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q24::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q24

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which statement best explains why x = 4 is not a function of x?
{
~A. It has no points#The vertical line has infinitely many points.
~B. It has no y-values#It has many y-values.
~C. It is horizontal#x = 4 is vertical, not horizontal.
=D. One input value has many output values#Correct. The input x = 4 pairs with many y-values.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_Q25::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L01_Q25

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which relationship is a function even though an output repeats?
{
=A. (1, 9), (2, 9), (3, 10)#Correct. Inputs are not repeated with different outputs.
~B. (1, 9), (1, 10), (2, 11)#Input 1 has two outputs.
~C. (3, 4), (3, 5), (4, 6)#Input 3 has two outputs.
~D. (2, 7), (2, 8), (2, 9)#Input 2 has three different outputs.
}

